<p>A book can feel impossible to outline because it is still carrying every version of itself at once. You have the subject, the scenes, the argument, the people you hope to reach, and perhaps a title that arrived before you knew what it meant. Put all of that on one page and it can look less like a beginning than a pile of obligations.</p>
<p>An outline gives the work somewhere to stand. It does not need to predict every chapter or remove surprise from the draft. Its practical job is smaller: to help you decide what the book is trying to do, what belongs inside it, and what a reader needs before they can follow you to the end.</p>
<p>This guide offers a flexible seven-step way to outline a book. It works for nonfiction, memoir, essays, and fiction with a clear central movement. Use the parts that help, leave room for discovery, and return to the plan whenever the draft begins to feel larger than the next useful decision.</p>
<h2>1. Start with the question beneath the book</h2>
<p>Before you make a chapter list, name the question that keeps the book alive. Not the category on a bookstore shelf, but the question a reader can feel. A book about leadership might be asking what responsibility looks like when old rules stop working. A book about identity might be asking which stories we inherit and which ones we can change. A novel might be asking what a person will trade to belong.</p>
<p>Write the question in plain language. Keep it short enough to remember without opening a file. When you are unsure whether a chapter, scene, example, or research trail belongs, this question gives you something better than instinct alone. It lets you ask, “Does this make the reader see the central problem more clearly?”</p>
<p>Patrick Okeke’s <a href="/books/blurred-lines">Blurred Lines</a> offers a useful reminder that large subjects become meaningful through the patterns people can actually recognize in their lives. Your question does not have to solve the world. It needs enough pressure to carry a reader through the pages that follow.</p>
