<h2>2. Write the promise you are making to the reader</h2>
<p>A reader opens a book hoping for a particular kind of change. They may want a clearer way to understand a subject, a story that moves them through uncertainty, a useful practice, or language for something they have felt but could not name. Your outline becomes easier once you can state what the reader will have by the end.</p>
<p>Try this sentence: “By the end of this book, the reader will be able to see, understand, feel, or do…” Finish it without exaggeration. “Become a better person” is too broad. “Recognize the habits that make a writing practice easier to return to” is specific enough to guide a real chapter.</p>
<p>The promise is not a marketing slogan. It is a private standard for your decisions. It keeps the book from becoming a collection of things you know but the reader does not yet need. If the promise changes after you begin, that is useful information. Update the outline so the whole book still moves in one direction.</p>
<h2>3. Choose the shape before you choose the details</h2>
<p>Most book outlines become unwieldy when writers try to decide everything at once. Start with the broad movement. Nonfiction often works through a problem, a set of ideas, a sequence of practices, or a change in perspective. A memoir may move through a life-changing period. Fiction often follows a desire, a complication, a series of choices, and a consequence.</p>
<p>Make four to seven rough sections. Give each a temporary name based on its job, not its final title. You might begin with “the problem,” “what people usually miss,” “a new way to see it,” “how to practice it,” and “what changes next.” A story might begin with “the ordinary world,” “the first disruption,” “the cost of continuing,” and “the choice that cannot be undone.”</p>
<p>Think of these sections as rooms in a house. You do not need the furniture yet. You need to know whether the reader can walk from one room to the next without finding a wall. This is where books become more coherent, because the order starts serving the experience instead of the order in which ideas happened to arrive.</p>
<h2>4. Turn each section into a chapter with one job</h2>
<p>Now make a possible chapter list. Give every chapter a single primary job. It might introduce a tension, explain a key idea, bring in a revealing example, complicate an easy answer, or show a reader what to try next. A chapter can do more than one thing, but it should have one clear reason for existing.</p>
<p>Under each working chapter title, write three brief notes: what the reader knows when the chapter begins, what they need to encounter inside it, and what should be clearer by the end. This keeps chapters from becoming storage containers for material you do not want to lose.</p>
<p>For a practical nonfiction book, you might also add one example, one question, or one action that belongs in each chapter. For a narrative book, add the change in the character’s situation or understanding. If you cannot name the turn, the chapter may still be a topic rather than a movement.</p>
